#!/bin/sh
# Kenstel KAP-110 board network configuration
#
# VERIFIED LIVE on the initramfs build: this topology reaches 192.168.1.1 from a
# PC plugged into lan0-lan3.
#
# Topology confirmed from the working initramfs console:
#   eth0   = CPU port, ipqess-edma c080000.ethernet (netifd enslaves it to br-lan)
#   lan0   = swport1  PHY [90000.mdio-1:00] QCA8072
#   lan1   = swport2  PHY [90000.mdio-1:01]
#   lan2   = swport3  PHY [90000.mdio-1:02]
#   lan3   = swport4  PHY [90000.mdio-1:03]
#   wan    = swport5  PHY [90000.mdio-1:04]   <- deliberately NOT bridged
#
# All four LAN ports plus the CPU port sit in one bridge. There is no WAN port
# and no NAT: this is an access point, not a router.
#
# NAMING: the stock (QSDK) firmware used eth1 with tagged VLANs (br-lan eth1.10,
# br-apman eth1.20, br-brman eth1.60, br-localman eth1). That does NOT translate
# to upstream OpenWrt, where DSA names the CPU port eth0 and the switch ports
# lan0-lan3/wan. Reproducing the VLAN design needs explicit vlan/untagged/pvid
# nodes in the DTS; without them the tagged interfaces come up but nothing
# forwards. Plain untagged LAN is the verified configuration.
#
# The vendor VLAN split (cloud management / backhaul) is intentionally dropped:
# the device is being taken off Kenstel's cloud, so those networks no longer
# have a purpose.

. /lib/functions.sh

ucidef_set_interfaces_lan_wan bridge lan "lan0" "lan1" "lan2" "lan3"
